<article id="paper">
<header>
<p class="kicker">Attention mechanisms / controlled empirical study</p>
<h1>Higher-Order Attention<br>Under Matched Training</h1>
<p class="subtitle">Token-loss gains, mixed controls, and reasoning limits in ordinary and 2-simplicial language models</p>
<p class="byline">Next-Gen Architecture Lab · CATL 21C Lab SCIAGI<br>Evidence synthesis prepared 13 October 2026 · Historical experiments, September–October 2026</p>
</header>
<section class="abstract" id="abstract">
<h2>Abstract</h2>
<p>Does explicitly attending to pairs of context tokens improve language-model learning? We examine exact 2-simplicial attention using recorded ordinary-attention controls, with a pretrained Limite-1B adaptation study as the principal experiment. Both arms insert 48 attention modules, train only the added modules for 2.000 billion supervised targets, and then unfreeze the backbone to reach 10 billion targets. They share data order and attention geometry; the simplicial arm has 2.57% more total parameters. On a fixed validation diagnostic containing 65,536 targets from 31 problems, simplicial attention has lower cross-entropy at all 66 shared saved checkpoints and finishes at 0.801842 versus 0.809566 nats per target. First observed crossings of cross-entropy 0.82 and 0.81 use 27.5% and 36.2% fewer cumulative targets, respectively. These descriptive token-efficiency gains do not establish compute or wall-clock savings. Native-context AIME 2026 evaluations yield 25/120 versus 27/120 correct responses after adapter warmup, and 19/120 versus 25/120 after full fine-tuning. Longer supervised training therefore improves the diagnostic loss without improving the observed reasoning score. Later aligned scratch prefixes favor simplicial attention, whereas earlier Qwen and DeepSeek controls favor ordinary attention. One training pair per setting, heterogeneous evaluation contracts, parameter differences, and an audited document-ending omission limit causal interpretation. The evidence supports context-dependent predictive gains and motivates replicated, parameter- and compute-controlled experiments with repaired data boundaries.</p>
</section>
<p class="keywords"><strong>Keywords</strong> &nbsp; higher-order attention · 2-simplicial attention · language modeling · mathematical reasoning · experimental controls</p>

<section id="introduction">
<h2><span class="num">1</span>Introduction</h2>
<p>Standard self-attention assigns a weight to each query–key interaction and combines the corresponding values <a class="cite" href="#r1">[1]</a>. A higher-order alternative scores a query against two context representations jointly. This construction offers a direct route to modeling relations involving three positions, but it also introduces additional projections, a larger interaction domain, and a different implementation cost. Expressive motivation alone does not determine whether that tradeoff improves a trained language model.</p>
<p>We study this question through the experiments of Next-Gen Architecture Lab. The principal comparison augments the same pretrained Limite-1B Base checkpoint with either ordinary or exact 2-simplicial attention before every native transformer block. Both variants retain the parent's attention, follow the same target-token order, and receive the same supervised target budget. This design controls several consequential sources of variation, while deliberately preserving each mechanism's native projection structure. It is consequently a geometry-matched comparison rather than a parameter- or compute-matched experiment.</p>
<p>The most consistent positive signal is lower predictive loss in the Limite continuation. Its interpretation requires care. The diagnostic repeatedly evaluates only 32 fixed windows; saved checkpoints share a training trajectory and cannot be counted as independent replications. Mathematical reasoning supplies a separate outcome: both added-attention variants improve the recorded zero-shot Base score after frozen-backbone warmup, but further full-weight supervised training does not improve their AIME mean correctness. A retrospective audit also identifies omission of most document endings from the historical training windows.</p>
<p>We make three empirical contributions. First, we state the implemented operator and the actual two-phase training contract, separating them from superseded proposals. Second, we reconstruct validation trajectories, observed threshold crossings, and complete benchmark endpoints from auditable aggregates. Third, we place the positive Limite result beside later positive scratch prefixes and earlier negative controls, preserving differences in data, scale, implementation, and completion status. The contribution is this empirical comparison and its limits; the attention mechanism itself follows prior work.</p>
<p class="note"><strong>Scope.</strong> This manuscript analyzes historical experiment records. It introduces no new training run, does not report peer review, and makes no claim of state-of-the-art reasoning or universal architectural superiority. The build rechecks plotted aggregates; it does not rerun the underlying models.</p>
</section>

<section id="related">
<h2><span class="num">2</span>Related work</h2>
<h3>Higher-order and relational attention</h3>
<p>Clift et al. introduced the 2-Simplicial Transformer for logical reasoning, using virtual entities and tensor-product value constructions <a class="cite" href="#r2">[2]</a>. Edge Transformers instead maintain persistent representations of pairs of input nodes and update them through triangular attention <a class="cite" href="#r4">[4]</a>. These approaches share an interest in relational composition, but their state spaces and operators differ from a token-state decoder with an added pair-attention module. Formal work on Strassen attention establishes expressivity separations for specific composition tasks in restricted, one-layer settings <a class="cite" href="#r5">[5]</a>. Such results motivate targeted diagnostics; they do not prove a limitation of our deep ordinary-attention control on mathematical reasoning.</p>
<p>The closest precedent is <em>Fast and Simplex</em>, which combines a trilinear score, joint normalization over key pairs, elementwise-product values, and tiled kernels <a class="cite" href="#r3">[3]</a>. Its windowed language-model experiments use a different architecture and insertion schedule, and report scale-dependent negative-log-likelihood outcomes, rather than generated-answer accuracy. Its 1B-active-parameter mixture-of-experts treatment shows no consistent gain, whereas its 2B- and 3.5B-active configurations improve the reported likelihoods. Our experiment uses a dense pretrained backbone, a short axis of 16, and attention insertions at all 48 blocks. We therefore test a related intervention rather than reproduce that model.</p>
<p>Linearized 2-simplicial attention replaces a global interaction axis with a random-feature approximation while retaining an explicit local axis <a class="cite" href="#r6">[6]</a>. Its findings depend on context and compute regime: under the reported 2k web analytic iso-FLOP budget, its KDA composition has mean accuracy 0.3446 against 0.3458 for the KDA hybrid, despite stronger results elsewhere. The study reports one seed. An exact finite-window operator and a random-feature approximation need separate numerical and empirical evaluation; gains from one do not establish gains from the other. We exclude historical runs with an invalid random-feature sampler from the present empirical synthesis.</p>
<h3>Adaptation, budgets, and execution</h3>
<p>Training newly inserted modules with a frozen pretrained network follows the broad adaptation strategy of Houlsby et al. <a class="cite" href="#r7">[7]</a>, although our modules are attention layers rather than bottleneck adapters. The subsequent full-weight phase changes both the inserted modules and the backbone. Disabling an adapter after this phase would not recover the original released model. Work on compute-optimal training motivates explicit accounting of parameters and training data <a class="cite" href="#r8">[8]</a>. In this study, matching supervised targets is one experimental control, not evidence of matched FLOPs. FlashAttention further illustrates how memory traffic and execution organization affect realized speed even for an exact attention computation <a class="cite" href="#r9">[9]</a>. We therefore separate token efficiency from kernel and end-to-end efficiency.</p>
<h3>Reasoning data and statistical evaluation</h3>
<p>Nemotron-Math provides long-form mathematical supervision across reasoning modes, including tool-assisted trajectories <a class="cite" href="#r10">[10]</a>. Our text-only rendering and subsequent window selection define a narrower training distribution. Limite Base and Violetto also occupy different points in the publisher's training lineage <a class="cite" href="#r11">[11]</a>, <a class="cite" href="#r12">[12]</a>; the released Violetto model is a contextual reference rather than a matched architecture control. Following the pass@<em>k</em> convention of Chen et al., averaging sampled response correctness estimates pass@1 <a class="cite" href="#r13">[13]</a>. Statistical comparisons must also respect dependence <a class="cite" href="#r14">[14]</a>: four responses to each of 30 problems are not 120 independent problem observations, and repeated checkpoints are not repeated training seeds.</p>
</section>

<section id="method">
<h2><span class="num">3</span>Attention mechanism and intervention</h2>
<h3>Exact joint attention over ordered key pairs</h3>
<p>For a query position <span class="math">i</span>, let <span class="math">J<sub>i</sub></span> contain the current position and up to 15 preceding positions. The long-axis set <span class="math">K<sub>i</sub></span> contains the current position and up to 1,024 preceding positions in local layers, or all causal positions in global layers. With projected and normalized queries and keys, the ordinary control computes</p>
<div class="equation" role="math" aria-label="Ordinary attention: softmax of scaled query key dot products over the causal long window, followed by a weighted sum of values."><div class="eqbody">{% filter mathsvg %}a_{ik}=\operatorname{softmax}_{k\in K_i}\!\left(\alpha q_i^{\top}k_k\right),\qquad o_i=\sum_{k\in K_i}a_{ik}v_k.{% endfilter %}</div><span class="eqno">(1)</span></div>
<p>The simplicial branch uses separate short- and long-axis key/value projections:</p>
<div class="equation" role="math" aria-label="Joint simplicial attention: trilinear score normalized over all ordered short and long key pairs, weighting the elementwise product of their values."><div class="eqbody"><div>{% filter mathsvg %}s_{ijk}=\alpha\sum_{d=1}^{128}q_{id}\,k^{S}_{jd}\,k^{L}_{kd},{% endfilter %}</div><div>{% filter mathsvg %}p_{ijk}=\frac{\exp(s_{ijk})}{\sum_{u\in J_i}\sum_{v\in K_i}\exp(s_{iuv})},{% endfilter %}</div><div>{% filter mathsvg %}o_i=\sum_{j\in J_i}\sum_{k\in K_i}p_{ijk}\left(v^{S}_{j}\odot v^{L}_{k}\right).{% endfilter %}</div></div><span class="eqno">(2)</span></div>
<p>The inherited softmax scale is <span class="math">α</span> = 0.1. Normalization is joint over the ordered Cartesian product <span class="math">J<sub>i</sub> × K<sub>i</sub></span>; diagonal pairs and the current position are allowed. There is no additional <span class="math">j ≤ k</span> constraint. The operation is neither two independent softmax attentions nor a determinant-based score. “Exact” identifies the intended softmax operator, in contrast to a random-feature approximation; the executed kernels still use finite-precision arithmetic.</p>
<h3>Insertion into the pretrained model</h3>
<p>Each of the 48 native blocks receives an attention prelude that RMS-normalizes the incoming hidden state and adds the projected attention output residually before native block processing. The native backbone attention remains in place. The new output projections start at zero, preserving the parent logits at initialization. Shared weights have matched initialization with base seed 42, incremented by layer index; the new QKV and short-axis projection matrices use Gaussian initialization with standard deviation 0.02. The parent is <code>paradigma-inc/limite-1b-base</code> at revision <code>cc612bafcd4acd8445936201694c8c41aea8e479</code>.</p>
<p>Both variants use width 1,280, ten query heads, two key/value heads, and head dimension 128. Each key/value group serves five query heads. The insertion preserves the local/global schedule, QK RMS normalization, local rotary positions, global attention without positional rotation, value embeddings, native gates, and other native attention mechanisms. Every fourth block is global.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><caption><strong>Table 1.</strong> Parameter accounting for the principal Limite comparison.</caption><thead><tr><th>Quantity</th><th class="numeric">Ordinary</th><th class="numeric">2-simplicial</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Shared pretrained parent</td><td class="numeric">1,035,254,176</td><td class="numeric">1,035,254,176</td></tr><tr><td>Added attention parameters</td><td class="numeric">188,806,080</td><td class="numeric">220,263,360</td></tr><tr><td>Total after insertion</td><td class="numeric">1,224,060,256</td><td class="numeric">1,255,517,536</td></tr><tr><td>Total relative to ordinary</td><td class="numeric">1.0000×</td><td class="numeric">1.0257×</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>The two additional short-axis projections account for 48 × 1,280 × (2 × 2 × 128) = 31,457,280 parameters, a 16.66% increase in adapter parameters and 2.57% in total parameters. The attention contraction scales with ∑<sub>i</sub>|<span class="math">J<sub>i</sub></span>||<span class="math">K<sub>i</sub></span>| rather than ∑<sub>i</sub>|<span class="math">K<sub>i</sub></span>|. The short-axis length of 16 must not be interpreted as a measured 16-fold whole-model cost.</p>
</section>

<section id="protocol">
<h2><span class="num">4</span>Experimental protocol</h2>
<h3>Two executed supervised phases</h3>
<p>The first phase trains only the inserted modules, with the parent frozen, through step 7,630 and 2,000,158,720 supervised next-token targets. The historical implementation used the native/Triton-CUDA attention path. Both checkpoints then undergo an explicit migration to TileLang, retaining adapter weights, AdamW state, random-number-generator state, and data order. The second phase unfreezes the full backbone and continues to exactly 10,000,000,000 cumulative supervised targets. This executed 2B-plus-8B schedule supersedes an earlier proposal for 10B of adapter-only training.</p>
<p>Each arm uses two nodes and 16 NVIDIA B300 GPUs. Sequence windows contain 2,048 targets, with global batch 128, microbatch eight per GPU, and no gradient accumulation. The adapter optimizer is AdamW with peak learning rate 10<sup>−4</sup>, β = (0.9, 0.95), zero weight decay, and the original global-token cosine schedule. The newly unfrozen backbone uses Transformer Engine FusedAdam with peak learning rate 10<sup>−5</sup>, a 100-step warmup, a cosine schedule over the remaining targets, and FP32 master weights and optimizer moments. The gradient norm cap is 1.0. Native BF16 matrices and FP32 small parameters are retained; distributed gradients are averaged in FP32.</p>
<h3>Training distribution and validation diagnostic</h3>
<p>The data derive from text-only Nemotron-Math-v2 reasoning trajectories, rendered with the shared Limite vocabulary and the native Violetto template. Unsupported tool conversations and system prompts are excluded under the preprocessing contract. The all-token next-token objective supervises prompts, chat serialization, and reasoning; it is not an assistant-only objective. Historical document-contained windows use neither padding nor cross-document attention. A normalized-problem hash assigns approximately 1% to validation, with common training order across variants and exact RL-heldout problem hashes excluded.</p>
<p>Every plotted Limite diagnostic scores the same 32 windows: 65,536 targets from 31 normalized problems, selected from a validation index of 138,550 windows. These are validation-split measurements, not training losses, but they cover a small fixed sample. The provenance audit verifies disjoint train/validation index prefixes and the split construction. It does not establish absence of semantic near-duplicates or pretrained-model exposure. We call the measurement <em>diagnostic cross-entropy</em> throughout.</p>
<h3>Reasoning benchmark and uncertainty</h3>
<p>AIME 2026 evaluation uses all 30 problems and four paired seeded samples per problem, temperature 0.6, top-p 0.95, and a 131,072-token total context. Each completion budget equals that context minus the actual prompt length; there is no artificial 16K or 32K evaluation cutoff. A strict answer grader checks explicit final answers after closed reasoning. Natural end-of-sequence and context exhaustion are recorded separately; reaching the limit is not itself a rule that invalidates an otherwise gradable answer.</p>
<div class="equation" role="math" aria-label="Mean pass at one is the average correctness over 30 problems and four samples for each problem."><div class="eqbody">{% filter mathsvg %}\widehat{p}_{1}=\frac{1}{30}\sum_{i=1}^{30}\frac{1}{4}\sum_{r=1}^{4}\mathbf{1}\!\left[\mathrm{response}_{ir}\ \mathrm{is\ correct}\right].{% endfilter %}</div><span class="eqno">(3)</span></div>
<p>This quantity is the mean pass@1 estimate, not pass@4. The supplied warmup marginal confidence intervals resample problems, preserving their four responses. Separately audited paired intervals resample the same 30 problems jointly across models, using 100,000 bootstrap draws with seed 20261008. Both describe evaluation-sample uncertainty conditional on the recorded checkpoints, not variation over training seeds. Endpoint totals alone cannot reconstruct these paired intervals; we use the retained paired aggregate audit and do not treat the 120 responses as independent problem observations. The Base evaluation is the project's historical zero-shot protocol, not a reproduction of the publisher's current few-shot Base harness.</p>
<h3>Supporting comparisons and inclusion policy</h3>
<p>We include the principal Limite pair, two later aligned exact-simplicial scratch prefixes, and earlier Qwen/DeepSeek ordinary-attention controls. All are single-seed comparisons. Each remains within its own metric, data, budget, and insertion contract. The supporting set is a purposive selection of mechanism-relevant recorded comparisons, not a preregistered systematic survey. We retain unfavorable results and identify incomplete budgets. Uncontrolled continuations, invalid random-feature runs, and triadic scratch attempts without a post-training held-out measurement cannot establish a paired effect and are excluded from quantitative synthesis.</p>
</section>

<section id="results">
<h2><span class="num">5</span>Results</h2>
<h3>5.1 &nbsp;Lower Limite diagnostic loss throughout continuation</h3>
<p>The 2B checkpoints begin the plotted continuation at diagnostic CE 0.860566 for ordinary attention and 0.845442 for simplicial attention. At the 10B endpoint the values are 0.809566 and 0.801842, respectively: a difference of −0.007724 nats per target, or approximately 0.95% relative to ordinary attention. Simplicial attention has lower loss at all 66 common saved points. The curve is consistent within this pair; those points are correlated observations of one trajectory, not 66 independent confirmations.</p>
<figure class="fig" id="fig-learning">{{ figures.learning | safe }}<figcaption><strong>Figure 1. Diagnostic learning curves for the Limite pair.</strong> All points score the same 32 validation windows. The lower panel shows paired CE differences at the 66 shared saved checkpoints. Cumulative targets include frozen-backbone warmup; full-weight continuation begins at approximately 2B. Connecting segments are visual guides, not additional measurements. No training-seed confidence band is available.</figcaption><div class="figure-links"><a href="figures/learning.svg">Vector SVG</a><a href="figures/learning.pdf">PDF figure</a><a href="figures/learning.csv">Plot data · CSV</a></div></figure>
<h3>5.2 &nbsp;Observed token efficiency depends on the comparison rule</h3>
<p>Using all 70 saved points per arm, we define a threshold crossing as the first recorded checkpoint with CE at or below the stated target. No interpolation, smoothing, or monotonicity assumption is used. Ordinary and simplicial first reach CE ≤ 0.82 at 5.242880B and 3.801088B targets, and CE ≤ 0.81 at 9.043968B and 5.767168B targets. These correspond to 27.5% and 36.2% fewer cumulative targets for the simplicial arm.</p>
<figure class="fig" id="fig-thresholds">{{ figures.thresholds | safe }}<figcaption><strong>Figure 2. First observed threshold crossings.</strong> Both arms use their first qualifying saved checkpoint, including the endpoint-CE threshold. Thresholds were chosen retrospectively; they have no seed-level uncertainty estimate. All budgets include warmup. Coarse checkpoint spacing limits the precision of the implied crossing times.</figcaption><div class="figure-links"><a href="figures/thresholds.svg">Vector SVG</a><a href="figures/thresholds.pdf">PDF figure</a><a href="figures/thresholds.csv">Plot data · CSV</a></div></figure>
<p>The ordinary 10B endpoint CE, 0.8095661402, is first reached by simplicial at 5.898240B targets and by ordinary at 9.568256B. Comparing both first crossings gives a 1.622× target-budget ratio, or 38.36% fewer targets. A different, fixed-endpoint question compares 10B with 5.898240B and gives 1.695×, or 41.02% fewer. Both calculations are descriptive and answer different questions. Neither ratio measures GPU-time savings: the arms use different kernels and graph optimizations, and no matched end-to-end time-to-threshold result is established here.</p>
<h3>5.3 &nbsp;Predictive loss and generated reasoning diverge</h3>
<p>At frozen-backbone warmup, the strict grader finds 5/120 correct Base responses, 25/120 for ordinary adapters, and 27/120 for simplicial adapters. Both trained variants improve the observed Base score substantially; the difference between the adapters is only two responses, or 1.67 percentage points. The supplied problem-bootstrap marginal intervals are broad: 10.0–33.3% for ordinary and 11.7–35.0% for simplicial. The paired 95% interval for simplicial minus ordinary is −5.83 to +9.17 percentage points; overlap of marginal intervals is not a paired significance test.</p>
<p>At 10B, ordinary and simplicial score 19/120 (15.83%) and 25/120 (20.83%). The descriptive gap is five percentage points, with a paired problem-bootstrap 95% interval of −1.67 to +11.67 percentage points. Both scores are below their respective 2B warmup scores despite lower diagnostic CE. Native-context exhaustion is common: 89/120 ordinary responses and 64/120 simplicial responses reach the context limit. These results demonstrate a separation between the measured teacher-forced objective and the recorded generated-answer outcome; they do not identify its unique cause.</p>
<figure class="fig" id="fig-aime">{{ figures.aime | safe }}<figcaption><strong>Figure 3. Reasoning accuracy and termination are distinct outcomes.</strong> Every point summarizes 30 problems × four samples. Warmup error bars are the supplied marginal 95% problem-cluster bootstrap intervals. SFT10B and historical RL400 bars use complete aggregate counts; marginal endpoint intervals are not shown. The paired SFT10B difference interval is reported separately in the text. RL400 names rollout-update steps, whose applied optimizer histories differ between arms. Context exhaustion is a separate event and is not the complement of correctness.</figcaption><div class="figure-links"><a href="figures/aime.svg">Vector SVG</a><a href="figures/aime.pdf">PDF figure</a><a href="figures/aime-warmup.csv">Warmup · CSV</a><a href="figures/aime-endpoints.csv">Endpoints · CSV</a></div></figure>
<p>The historical full-weight RL continuation gives 15/120 for ordinary and 25/120 for simplicial after 400 rollout-update steps. Neither improves its own SFT10B mean score. Context exhaustion falls to 2/120 and 5/120, respectively, showing that improved termination alone does not establish improved reasoning. These RL runs used different applied-update histories and a 16,384-token training completion cap with additional length penalties. They are secondary observations, not a fresh matched architecture test. The later native-context restart and current verl-backed agentic pipeline did not produce these historical endpoints.</p>
<p>For context, the released Violetto checkpoint scores 111/120 (92.50%) under the project's endpoint evaluation. Its different training lineage makes it unsuitable as a causal attention control. It nevertheless shows that the low scores of the experimental branches should not be presented as a general limit of this model family.</p>
<h3>5.4 &nbsp;Later aligned scratch prefixes also favor simplicial attention</h3>
<p>The <code>d128</code> and <code>d640</code> labels denote backbone widths 128 and 640, each with depth 20. Both retain native backbone attention and add branches at eight sites, with eight query heads, two key/value heads, and head dimension 16. Ordinary branches use a causal window of 512; simplicial branches use long and short windows of 512 and 32. Within each pair, training uses the same FineWeb-Edu document stream and tokenizer, context 2,048, 64 windows per update, seed 42, and 16 B300 GPUs per arm. Engram parameters use Sinkhorn updates and other parameters use FP32-factored Adafactor. Each validation point scores one million targets. This token-level CE has a different data and model contract from the Limite diagnostic.</p>
<p>Two later scratch pairs, identified by the campaign labels <code>d128</code> and <code>d640</code>, provide a second positive setting. Within each pair, recorded scientific contracts share source revision, data, optimizer settings, and shared-weight initialization; the simplicial projection parameters and variant identity differ. At the common approximately 2B-target checkpoint, CE is 3.787448 versus 3.761292 for <code>d128</code> and 3.132243 versus 3.120069 for <code>d640</code>, favoring simplicial attention.</p>
<figure class="fig" id="fig-scratch">{{ figures.scratch | safe }}<figcaption><strong>Figure 4. Aligned scratch comparisons at shared validation checkpoints.</strong> Curves are truncated to common observed coverage within each pair. Each column represents one seed and a distinct model size; the lower panels show paired CE differences. The equal initialization points are omitted from the plots and retained in the companion CSV. Neither pair completes its intended 10B budget. Absolute CE across columns is not a controlled estimate of the attention effect; compare the two variants within each column. These are exact-simplicial runs, distinct from later triadic-kernel qualification attempts.</figcaption><div class="figure-links"><a href="figures/scratch.svg">Vector SVG</a><a href="figures/scratch.pdf">PDF figure</a><a href="figures/scratch.csv">Plot data · CSV</a></div></figure>
<p>At the latest common validation, <code>d128</code> reaches 2.600057B targets with CE 3.706972 ordinary versus 3.683721 simplicial; <code>d640</code> reaches 2.300021B with CE 3.096474 versus 3.086271. Simplicial loss is lower at 22/26 and 21/23 shared nonzero validation points, respectively. The total parameter counts are approximately 0.570B and 5.186B, including large embedding/memory allocations; the campaign labels should not be read as total model sizes. Incomplete budgets, one seed, and only two configurations preclude a fitted scaling law or a convergence claim.</p>
<h3>5.5 &nbsp;Earlier controls give a different result</h3>
<p>The earlier Qwen W320 scratch pilot compares global ordinary, local ordinary, and simplicial attention at 3.003B tokens. Final CE is 3.663525, 3.641465, and 3.649627, respectively. Simplicial improves on the global arm but loses to the ordinary local arm. The local control is therefore necessary: comparison to the global arm alone would overstate the evidence for pair interactions.</p>
<p>In the earlier DeepSeek adaptation study, ordinary attention wins the matched frozen-adapter endpoint (0.798750 versus 0.800121) and the final full-weight comparison (0.700900 versus 0.701895). The latter scores one million targets in 200 windows from 162 problems at 756,364,650 matched training targets. Its problem-cluster bootstrap interval for simplicial minus ordinary CE is [+0.000745, +0.001269]. This measures evaluation-sample uncertainty for one training pair. The smaller periodic diagnostic favored simplicial at 22/27 shared points, yet the larger final evaluation reversed that ranking—direct evidence for caution when interpreting a fixed small validator.</p>
<figure class="fig" id="fig-controls">{{ figures.controls | safe }}<figcaption><strong>Figure 5. Earlier within-study controls.</strong> The Qwen panel reports absolute CE for three arms under one shared contract. The DeepSeek panel reports the simplicial-minus-ordinary CE difference divided by ordinary CE; positive values favor ordinary attention. Each study uses its own common budget and evaluation contract. The DeepSeek full-weight interval is the recorded 95% problem-cluster bootstrap interval transformed by the same fixed denominator; other rows lack comparable uncertainty. No pooled effect or meta-analytic average is computed.</figcaption><div class="figure-links"><a href="figures/controls.svg">Vector SVG</a><a href="figures/controls.pdf">PDF figure</a><a href="figures/controls.csv">Plot data · CSV</a></div></figure>
<p>The original DeepSeek scratch pair also favors ordinary attention: CE 4.122233 versus 4.160268 at 240.875M targets, only 2.41% of its planned 10B budget. Its architecture differs substantially from the later aligned pairs, so these results are not repeated trials of one intervention. In the full-weight adaptation timing window, mean updates take 94.412 seconds for ordinary and 109.774 seconds for simplicial, excluding evaluation and checkpoint pauses. The measured result favors ordinary throughput in that workload; it cannot be transferred to Limite or treated as a universal kernel ranking.</p>
</section>

<section id="discussion">
<h2><span class="num">6</span>Discussion and limitations</h2>
<h3>What the evidence supports</h3>
<p>The principal positive result is a persistent diagnostic-loss advantage for the Limite simplicial arm under a shared target-token schedule. Later aligned scratch prefixes suggest the phenomenon is not confined to pretrained adaptation. Earlier controls demonstrate that it is not universal. This heterogeneity is compatible with interactions among backbone, placement, data distribution, parameter allocation, and optimization; the current comparisons do not isolate which interaction matters.</p>
<p>We cannot attribute the Limite difference uniquely to the trilinear operator. The treatment adds 31.46M parameters and uses a different interaction domain. A parameter-matched ordinary control is missing. Equal supervised targets also leave FLOPs and wall-clock cost unequal. A useful next comparison would separately test equal target budgets, equal parameter budgets, and equal measured compute budgets; collapsing these into one “matched” label would obscure the estimand.</p>
<h3>A material data-boundary defect</h3>
<p>The historical fixed-window index discards incomplete document tails and excludes short documents. An audit of 64 sampled shard prefixes covers 112,773 documents, including 83,403 long documents and 29,370 excluded short ones. Metadata arithmetic estimates that only 104/83,403 terminal-EOS positions survive, assuming EOS occurs at the penultimate position. A direct binary spot-check finds EOS in 1,003 inspected endings, of which only four are retained. These are distinct checks, not an exhaustive token-level count over all 83,403 documents.</p>
<p>Omitting solution endings can plausibly weaken supervision for final answers and termination. High observed context exhaustion is consistent with that hypothesis, but no matched repair experiment establishes causality. The available overlap-final-window policy changes the data contract and does not repair historical checkpoints. A repaired comparison should retain endings, account for overlapped supervision, and explicitly decide how to include short documents.</p>
<h3>Statistical and reproducibility limits</h3>
<p>Each included setting has one training seed pair. Checkpoint-to-checkpoint consistency does not quantify seed variance, and the AIME benchmark contains only 30 independent problem clusters. Thresholds are selected retrospectively. The fixed Limite diagnostic covers 31 problems, while benchmark contamination, near-duplicates, and pretrained exposure have not been comprehensively audited. The source evidence does not justify a seed-level confidence interval, a causal mediation analysis of termination, or a claim of general reasoning improvement.</p>
<p>Recorded numerical admissions support the tested implementations: independent FP32 attention references, finite optimizer updates, zero-output initialization checks, and checkpoint restoration. They do not establish equivalence for every shape, precision, context, or backend. Normal and simplicial also received different execution optimizations during Limite continuation. New kernel speedups require their own whole-model measurements.</p>
<p>Finally, compact public aggregates permit reconstruction of these figures, but raw completions, model weights, data payloads, and historical source archives remain in team storage. The public repository is a curated snapshot, not the complete historical commit graph. The supporting experiments are selected from a broader research program and should not be interpreted as an exhaustive, publication-bias-free sample.</p>
<h3>Experiments needed for a stronger claim</h3>
<p>A decisive follow-up should replicate both arms with independent training seeds; add a parameter-matched ordinary attention control; freeze a repaired data contract and a larger validation set; preregister benchmark endpoints and loss thresholds; and measure end-to-end time and energy to a common quality target. A mechanism-specific claim would additionally benefit from controlled short-axis length and placement ablations and from relational tasks whose required dependencies are known. These are proposed experiments, not completed results.</p>
</section>

<section id="conclusion">
<h2><span class="num">7</span>Conclusion</h2>
<p>Exact 2-simplicial attention yields a consistent descriptive signal in the recorded Limite pair: lower small-sample validation CE throughout continuation and earlier observed crossings of selected loss thresholds. Two later scratch prefixes also favor it. Earlier ordinary-attention controls, however, show that the effect depends on the experimental setting, and AIME results do not establish a reliable architectural reasoning advantage. The appropriate conclusion is a bounded empirical one: higher-order attention merits further controlled study, with predictive loss, generated correctness, termination, and measured compute reported as separate outcomes.</p>
</section>

